    "answer": {
      "short": "$a=3$, $b=2$.\n\nLet $A=2^a$ and $B=3^b$; solve the linear system, then equate powers.",
      "worked": "Let $A=2^a$ and $B=3^b$. The product law gives\n$$\\begin{align*}A+B&=17\\\\2A-3B&=-11.\\end{align*}$$\nSubtract the second equation from twice the first.\n$$\\begin{align*}2(A+B)-(2A-3B)&=34-(-11)\\\\5B&=45\\\\B&=9\\\\A+9&=17\\\\A&=8.\\end{align*}$$\nHence\n$$\\begin{align*}2^a&=2^3\\\\a&=3\\\\3^b&=3^2\\\\b&=2.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
